!
! Line 14's CNOP prints NO location and NO binary.  The proof that
! it emitted nothing is arithmetic and is on the page: line 13 is a
! half word at 221523.40, so the counter stands at 221524.00 -- a
! word boundary -- and line 15 PRINTS 221524.00.  A CNOP at a word
! boundary has nothing to align.  (printed p.184 line 31 is the
! other case: a CNOP at .40 emits a half word and prints
! "000000.30 00".)
!

!SYM JBSPX  221520.40
!SYM JBSPC  221524.00
!SYM JSEOPT 221525.00
!SYM JSEOPZ 221531.00
!SYM JKCCW  221540.00
!SYM JNEWTE 221541.00
!
!CHECK 221515.00 | 221512.40 80 | line 1 SIC,KKRET -TO REJECT ROUTINE -- KKRET is an external READ OFF THIS ROW, and a SIC carries a full 24-bit bit address, so the .40 is a reading and not a convention (workflow "a VFL operand or a SIC reads every bit")
!CHECK 221515.40 | 221505.10 00 | line 2 B,KIOREJ -- external; a plain B shows only bit 32, so this checks the half word (0o00 OR 0o10) and the branch byte 00, not the bit
!CHECK 221516.00 | 221517.03 01 | line 3 LVI,$1,$+1.0 -TO SEOP TEST -- $ is this row's 221516.00 so $+1.0 is 221517.00, a REAL address check; .03 is 2J+1 for J=1 and the byte is 01, NOT the C1 the drum prints (glyph call 1)
!CHECK 221516.40 | 221525.10 00 | line 4 B,JSEOPT -- JSEOPT is THIS page's line 27 at 221525.00, so this pins the label
!CHECK 221517.00 | 221520.50 00 | line 5 B,JBSPX -EPGK IF AT LOAD POINT -- JBSPX is THIS page's line 8 at 221520.40: 0o40 OR 0o10 = 0o50
!CHECK 221517.40 | 221521.10 00 | line 6 B,JBSPX+.32 -UK NOT ACCEPTABLE -- JBSPX+32 bits is 221521.00, which is the four-way fan's second arm; the bare-decimal ".32" printing as the next word is the workflow's bare-literal rule put to work
!CHECK 221520.00 | 000000.30 00 | line 7 NOP -EE OK
!CHECK 221520.40 | 221520.50 00 | line 8 JBSPX B,$ -NORMAL EXIT -- identical to line 5's word, which is what makes line 5 a check of the label rather than of the byte alone
!CHECK 221521.00 | 000011.77 80 221274.34 00 | line 9 BZB,$R+0.63,K SOS -BR. IF UK ALONE TO SETUP ERR -- $R is word 0o11 and +0.63 is bit 63 = 0o77, so the bit-address conversion is checked outright; K SOS is the landed external 221274.0 and the branch half shows only bit 32
!CHECK 221522.00 | 221514.72 48 | line 10 CB,$13,JBSPE+.32 -REISSUE IF UK ALONE -- 0o72 is 0o40 (bit 32, from the +.32) OR 2J = 26 = 0o32 for J=13, so this row checks the index encoding AND the half word; JBSPE is an external on printed p.191
!CHECK 221522.40 | 003406.35 01 | line 11 LVI,$14,SREJ -- .35 is 2J+1 = 29 for J=14 with bit 32 CLEAR, which is what confirms the landed SREJ at 003406.0; byte 01 (glyph call 1)
!CHECK 221523.00 | 217451.40 80 | line 12 SIC,SDISIC -TO ERROR CONTROL -- SDISIC is landed at 217451.40 by four other decks and a SIC reads every bit, so this row CHECKS that landed value
!CHECK 221523.40 | 217372.10 00 | line 13 B,SDISP -- location carries a second asterisk; SDISP is landed at 217372.0 and a B shows only bit 32
!CHECK 221524.00 | 000000.00 8E 000176.15 00 | line 15 JBSPC CTL(SEOP),0.0($14),(8)176.0 -BSP COMMAND -- 8E's low nibble is index $14; .15 is the CTLSEOP code; 0o176 is the backspace control code written in STRAP's ADDRESS form, which the assembler keeps word-granular
!CHECK 221525.00 | 000036.35 02 | line 27 J SEOP T LCI,14,K REP -- K REP is the landed COUNT 30 = 0o36, so the address field is the VALUE and not an address; .35 is 2J+1 for J=14 and 02 is the LCI byte.  Measured: the dds makes no difference to this row
!CHECK 221525.40 | 000000.00 8E 221540.21 00 | line 28 CCW,0.0($14),JKCCW -- JKCCW is THIS page's line 43 at 221540.00, so this is a real address check; .21 is the CCW code, 8E the $14 index
!CHECK 221526.40 | 221531.03 44 | line 29 BZEKJZ,JSEOPZ -- JSEOPZ is THIS page's line 34 at 221531.00; byte 44 is the branch-on-zero-EKJ-and-reset code and the volume OCR of another page prints the same mnemonic with the same byte (glyph call 4)
!CHECK 221527.00 | 221525.74 48 | line 30 CB,$14,JSEOPT+.32 -- JSEOPT+32 bits is 221525.40; 0o74 is 0o40 OR 2J = 28 = 0o34 for J=14, so this row checks the label AND the index together
!CHECK 221527.40 | 003406.35 01 | line 31 LVI,$14,SREJ -IF EXCHANGE BAD -- the same card as line 11, printed a second time; two identical words from two cards is the page's own self-check on the byte
!CHECK 221530.00 | 217451.40 80 | line 32 SIC,SDISIC -- the same card as line 12 (and LG000195, the same SERIAL, with line 33)
!CHECK 221530.40 | 217372.10 00 | line 33 B,SDISP -- the same statement as line 13 and NO second asterisk, which is what refutes a statement-shaped rule for the mark
!CHECK 221531.00 | 221540.30 80 221525.34 02 | line 34 JSEOPZ BB,JKCCW+.24,JSEOPT -LOOP ON SEOP BIT UNTIL I/O IS DONE -- BB's first operand is a 24-BIT BIT ADDRESS, so JKCCW+24 bits prints .30 and the row reads JKCCW to the bit; the branch half carries JSEOPT
!CHECK 221532.00 | 221540.23 80 004000.20 50 | line 35 L(BU,4),JKCCW+.19 -EPGK,UK,EE,EOP BITS -- a VFL OPERAND, so JKCCW+19 bits prints .23 bit-exactly; the second half carries length 4 and the load byte 50
!CHECK 221533.00 | 000001.74 C3 | line 36 BRZ,1.32($1) -NEVER HAVE EOP ALONE -- 0o74 is 0o40 (bit 32 of the 1.32) OR the BRZ code 0o34; THE BYTE REALLY IS C3 and not 03, which is why no blanket round-glyph rule was applied on this page
!CHECK 221533.40 | 221313.40 80 | line 37 SIC,KCBJR -RELEASE -- KCBJR is landed at 221313.40 by pp.125/126/184 and a SIC reads every bit, so this row CHECKS that value; the spelling is p.184's GLYPH CALL (8)
!CHECK 221534.00 | 221302.50 00 | line 38 B,KCLEAR -THE CHANNEL -- KCLEAR is landed at 221302.40: 0o40 OR 0o10 = 0o50, so the half word is checked
!CHECK 221534.40 | 221540.23 80 004000.20 50 | line 39 L(BU,4),JKCCW.19 -- PRINTED WITHOUT A PLUS SIGN and it compiles identically to line 35; handed to strap_asm in that form and accepted
!CHECK 221535.40 | 000011.74 80 000000.34 03 | line 40 BB,$R+.60,0.0($1) -IF EPGK -- bit 60 of $R is 0o74, so the decimal-to-octal bit conversion is checked; the branch half is 0.0 indexed by $1 and prints the BB code 0o34 with byte 03
!CHECK 221536.40 | 000011.75 80 000000.74 03 | line 41 BB,$R+.61,.32($1) -IF UK -- location carries a second asterisk; bit 61 is 0o75, and the branch half's 0o74 is 0o40 (from the .32) OR the same 0o34 line 40 prints, so the two rows check each other
!CHECK 221537.40 | 000001.10 01 | line 42 B,1.0($1) -MUST BE EE OR EE,EOP -- byte 01 is B indexed by $1 (B unindexed prints 00, printed p.184 line 1); the drum prints the 0 as a C here
!CHECK 221540.00 | 000000.00+ 000 000000 000000 | line 43 JKCCW CW,0 -COPIED CW -- the same shape printed p.148 line 9 prints for "MCCW1 CW,0"; lines 28, 34, 35 and 39 all reference this word and agree on 221540.00
!CHECK 221541.00 | 000021.00 80 221617.06 A0 | line 51 JNEWTE TI,3,$1,JNEWXR -- $1 is index register 1, whose cell is word 0o21, which is the first half word; JNEWXR is an external read off the second half (half word only).  THE BYTE IS A0, read at 1200 dpi after 300 dpi said AC; I2 = 0 because the second operand carries no index
